  "prediction": "**Rescue Mission: Swedish Professor Helps Save Student's Family from ISIS**\n\nIn a remarkable story of courage and determination, Swedish professor Charlotta Turner helped rescue her graduate student Firas Jumaah's family from the clutches of ISIS in Iraq in 2014. Jumaah, a member of the Yazidi minority, had been working on his PhD at Lund University in southern Sweden when he received a text message from his wife, warning him of the growing violence in their home country.\n\nJumaah had initially stayed behind to complete his lab work, but when he returned to Iraq for a family wedding, he found himself in the midst of a deadly conflict. His family was facing a life-and-death situation, and Jumaah sent a desperate text message to his supervisor, Turner, asking her to remove him from the doctoral program if he wasn't back in Sweden within a week.\n\nTurner, a professor of analytical chemistry, was shocked by the message and quickly called Jumaah to discuss the situation. She soon realized that his family was in grave danger and that Jumaah was planning to go back to Iraq to rescue them. However, with borders closed and ISIS advancing, Jumaah's plan was put on hold.\n\nUndeterred, Turner took matters into her own hands and approached the university's security chief, Per Gustafson, who found a company that could go in with armed men and rescue Jumaah and his family. Over a few days of intense activity, the rescue operation was planned and executed, with two Landcruisers carrying four heavily-armed mercenaries arriving at the scene to extract Jumaah and his family.\n\nThe family was eventually taken to Erbil Airport and flown to safety, with Jumaah later completing his PhD and now working for a pharmaceuticals company in Malmö. The family has almost finished paying the university back for the rescue operation, which was a unique event in the university's history.\n\nTurner's actions were driven by her desire to help her student and his family, and she has spoken about the experience, saying, \"It was very spontaneous. For me, it was obvious that I should help and bring them home.\" The story is a testament to the power of human kindness and determination in the face of adversity.",
